 using or the specific reagents that type  of information can be found in our  application notes as well as a variety  of other resources that can be found at  biomech Beckman calm and with that I'm  happy to take any questions thank you  dr. Costa for that informative  presentation so dis time for Q&amp;A if you  have a question you'd like to ask dr.  Kowalski please do so now just click on  the green to an a button at the lower  left of the presentation window type  your question into the box that appears  on your screen and click on the send  button will answer as many questions as  we have time for my first question is  having successfully automated cell  culture without the tilting help like  optimizing captioning that's a great  question and again it might necessarily  depend on a given workflow for example  the large one well places they're called  that would potentially be a little  trickier to kind of work our way around  but the six wall plate we are able to  ask rate around the circumference and  that would be a just as effective way of  removing the media without disturbing  the cells it might just take a little  longer to kind of execute that process  so there's definitely a time in a place  where the tilting up where it provides  value but certainly not required for  most of the place up to the very large  maybe one well place or for example the  auto flasks which really only have a  single point of entry that's where again  the tilting out might be required  otherwise it may just have benefits for  some of the place that probably isn't  necessary how hard is it to adapt the  automation to different workflows that's  also a good question so the the  workflows I've shown today we're all  essentially done using the same deck but  obviously it depends what system you'd  be working on having the i7 hybrid sort  of provides you with the most  flexibility but there are certain  occasions where again depending on your  workloads if things are changing  dramatically you can imagine possibly  needing to change what devices are  integrated to the system one of the nice  things on the iseries is again there's  more universality of the integration so  those do become more easily swapped in  and out but when it comes to just  changing the positions on the deck for  example  needed more heating and cooling  positions or more shaking positions or  had some direct integration that change  to your deck configuration that's very  easily done that's basically just  popping out the current else positions  putting on the next positions and then  doing a quick otto frame using our tools  that are available when it comes to  possibly swapping out heads for example  you can change between the 96 and the  384 multi-channel head that process is  basically just unscrewing four screws  and screwing them back in so even  changing for that type of workflow is  very rapid so does it's basically built  and designed to have high levels of  flexibility so again barring some sort  of massive reconstruction of of your  needs it should be very quickly able to  switch between workflows or otherwise  you may be able to just automate a  number of workflows on a single  configuration how do you handle  contamination yeah so this comes up a  lot actually so in seven years of doing  this I've only run into it once and my  way of dealing with it was to throw out  the contaminated plate I really didn't  have any other problems that's kind of  one of the benefits that in theory if  things are working right you shouldn't  really be spreading contamination so if  you want to treat it the way I would  treat a contaminated incubator you can  spray things down with ethanol but just  throwing away the plate was sufficient  for me I didn't really have any  continuing problems after that there was  not really any need to do anything  extreme but there are certainly you know  we do have information on chemical  compatibility if people really need more  information about sterilization but  honestly for me everything stays pretty  well contained within the place so  throwing it out took care of my problem  after waiting for additional questions  to come in i would like to once again  thank dr. Kowalski for his presentation  do you have any final comments again  just want to thank everyone for  attending today obviously we're  available for additional information and  when it comes to cellular workflows or  obviously my colleagues have presented  on some genomic workflows as well so  this is obviously just one aspect of  what you can do with automation and what  you can do with the biomech guys  so we're happy to always provide more  additional information as required thank  you I would also like to thank our  sponsor beckman coulter life sciences  for making today's educational webcast  possible before we go I want to let  everyone know that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through october twenty of 2017 you'll  receive an email from laggards letting  you knowing this webcast will be  available for replay please share that  announcement with your colleagues you  may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us we hope to see you again soon bye
 

